  "description": "Recalibrate channel-wise feature responses using global average pooling squeeze followed by two-layer bottleneck excitation.\n\nSqueeze-and-Excitation blocks explicitly model interdependencies between convolutional feature channels, adaptively emphasizing informative features.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{s} = \\frac{1}{HW} \\sum_{h, w} \\mathbf{X}_{:, h, w}, \\quad \\mathbf{e} = \\sigma(\\mathbf{W}_2 \\text{ReLU}(\\mathbf{W}_1 \\mathbf{s})), \\quad \\tilde{\\mathbf{X}} = \\mathbf{e} \\odot \\mathbf{X}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute global spatial average pooling across height and width per channel\n2. Pass channel descriptor through bottleneck MLP with reduction ratio and apply Sigmoid gating\n3. Multiply input feature channels by computed scale vector and round to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nNested 3D list of shape (C, H, W) representing channel-recalibrated activations.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Squeeze-and-Excitation Block\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNeural networks achieve their remarkable expressive power by stacking linear layers interspersed with non-linear activation functions and normalization layers. Without non-linear activations, any deep network would collapse into a single shallow linear regression. **Squeeze-and-Excitation Block** ensures signals propagate cleanly through dozens or hundreds of layers without vanishing or exploding.\n\nSqueeze-and-Excitation blocks explicitly model interdependencies between convolutional feature channels, adaptively emphasizing informative features. In this problem, the goal is to: recalibrate channel-wise feature responses using global average pooling squeeze followed by two-layer bottleneck excitation.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{s} = \\frac{1}{HW} \\sum_{h, w} \\mathbf{X}_{:, h, w}, \\quad \\mathbf{e} = \\sigma(\\mathbf{W}_2 \\text{ReLU}(\\mathbf{W}_1 \\mathbf{s})), \\quad \\tilde{\\mathbf{X}} = \\mathbf{e} \\odot \\mathbf{X}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute global spatial average pooling across height and width per channel\n2. **Phase 2**: Pass channel descriptor through bottleneck MLP with reduction ratio and apply Sigmoid gating\n3. **Phase 3**: Multiply input feature channels by computed scale vector and round to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Gradient Flow**: Carefully designed activation functions (like ReLU, GELU, and Swish) maintain strong gradient signals during backpropagation.\n* **Internal Covariate Shift**: Normalization techniques (like LayerNorm and RMSNorm) standardize hidden states across channels or batch dimensions, allowing models to train stably at higher learning rates.\n* **Hardware Friendly**: Modern deep learning frameworks implement these layers using fused GPU kernels to reduce memory round-trips.",
  "examples": [
